  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nCurrent analysis of the mid levels shows a low pressure system\nmoving through northern MT stretching southward. Ensembles keep\nthis low in northern MT and stretches better energy to areas of\nWY south. This pattern is expected to favor the mountain and \nfoothill areas as well as the plains of southeast MT. As cooler \nair pushes in this evening and winds switch to the \nnorth/northwest, this will be the brief window for the Red Lodge \nand area foothills to receive up to a few inches of snow. \nCurrently, there is about a 50% chance for these areas to get at \nleast two inches of snow. About a 50-80% chance exists for the \nBighorn foothills west and southwest of Sheridan. Accumulations \nacross the lower elevations will be light and elevation dependent \n(higher elevation, higher amount). Watch the forecast and check \nroad conditions if traveling in these areas. Areas mountains can \nexpect to see around 5-10 inches of additional snowfall. Snow is \nexpected to decrease through the evening and overnight. Trailing \nenergy behind the low will lead to scattered rain/snow showers \nFriday with an isolated thunderstorm also possible. \n\nFor the weekend through mid week, ensembles are in good agreement\nfor ridging bringing in warm and dry conditions. They then depict\ncool and unsettled weather sometime late Tuesday into Wednesday.\nStill some uncertainty with this system, so watch the forecast as\nthe time approaches. \n\nHigh temperatures will be cooler in the 30s and 40s Friday,\nwarming through the week into the 50s-70s and low 80s by Tuesday-\nWednesday. TS",
